; Managed by NimVor (installer/install.sh).
;
; NimVor's greylisting and mail limits are Postfix policy services started
; by systemd socket units (nimvor-greylist.socket, nimvor-mailrate.socket).
; Their sockets live in Postfix's private folder, where smtpd reaches them:
; /var/spool/postfix/private (postfix_private_t).
;
; 1. The distribution policy does not let systemd (init_t) create a socket
;    there ("avc: denied { add_name } ... comm=systemd ... tclass=dir"), so
;    the socket units failed. The sockets take the folder's type, as
;    Postfix's own do.
; 2. The services run, like the agent, as unconfined_service_t (they are
;    confined by their systemd sandbox instead: dynamic user, no
;    capabilities, read-only system), and smtpd may not connect to such a
;    socket ("avc: denied { connectto } ... comm=smtpd ...
;    tcontext=system_u:system_r:unconfined_service_t"): Postfix could not
;    ask them, so greylisting and mail limits had no effect.
;
; This allows exactly these, nothing else. SELinux stays enforcing.
(allow init_t postfix_private_t (dir (search getattr write add_name remove_name)))
(allow init_t postfix_private_t (sock_file (create unlink getattr setattr)))
(allow postfix_smtpd_t unconfined_service_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))
